<DocsShell title="Web and native, one contract — Texuli's architecture" description="One manifest projects to TypeScript and Kotlin: the spec seam that keeps web and native interfaces portable. What exists today, honestly." {toc} {jsonLd} demoNav={false}>
  <h2 id="why-both">Why both technologies</h2>
  <p>
    Texuli uses a different runtime on each side on purpose, and the reason is the same
    for both: each platform deserves the technology that fits it, not a compromise shared
    between them.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Svelte 5 for the web.</strong> The compiler carries the framework: components
      compile away, state is explicit through runes, and size is budgeted — <code>pnpm size</code> and the
      site-perf audit measure packages and pages, and the entries still over budget are tracked
      in the open. A runtime chosen with constrained devices, including phones, in mind.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Kotlin Multiplatform and Compose for native.</strong> One language that can
      reach desktop, iOS and Android. Today the Compose renderer runs on the JVM (desktop), the
      spec models also compile for iOS, and Android is pending a decision.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This is not one framework ported onto the other: Texuli does not emulate Compose in the
    browser or transpile Svelte to Kotlin. Each runtime stays the native idiom of its
    platform, and what Texuli owns is the contract between them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The long-form reasoning lives in the guide <a href="/guides/why-svelte-kotlin/">Why Svelte and Kotlin</a>;
    the native story has its own landing at <a href="/native/">Texuli native</a>.
  </p>

  <div id="how-layered">
    <LayerGraph />
    <TexelLadder />
  </div>

  <h2 id="one-contract">One contract, two projections</h2>
  <p>
    The seam is the spec contract: one authored catalog manifest, two generated projections —
    TypeScript under <code>packages/spec/generated/typescript</code> and Kotlin under
    <code>packages/spec/generated/kotlin</code>. The demo below walks one component through
    that seam.
  </p>
  <WebCorrelation />

  <div id="portability">
    <PortabilityDeepDive />
  </div>

  <h2 id="what-exists-today">What exists today</h2>
  <p>
    The web runtime ships: all 15 packages are on npm as <code>0.1.0-next.1</code> under the
    dist-tag <code>next</code> (CI publishes with provenance; <code>latest</code> is reserved
    for 1.0.0). The native side is in development: the Compose renderer is consumed from
    source in this repository and nothing is published to Maven Central yet. The table below
    states the capability status per side, with the evidence for each row.
  </p>
  <ParityTable />
</DocsShell>
